# Instant UI and Backend Sync

Purpose: every UI change involving shared backend data must update the acting
user immediately and converge across authorized sessions through the standard
realtime pipeline.

## Mandatory end-to-end flow

1. Widgets read authoritative UI data only from Riverpod and delegate actions to
   controllers; widgets never call APIs or maintain competing server-data copies.
2. Controllers write through repositories over HTTP. WebSockets are server-to-client
   synchronization, never a mutation transport.
3. On successful persistence (`saved == true`), patch every affected Riverpod slice
   immediately from the response or a typed local delta: rows, details, summaries,
   cards, alerts, and navigation badges. Cancel or failure must not patch or refresh.
4. After commit and audit, the backend publishes a scoped domain event for every
   user-visible shared mutation. Opting out requires an explicit documented reason.
5. Controllers subscribe with the relevant `RealtimeEventGroups` and
   `listenForRealtimeRefresh`, including audit CRUD events where applicable.
6. Reconcile through `WorkspaceSyncEngine`: apply a safe typed `RealtimeDelta`, or
   run the smallest `WorkspaceRefreshPlan` HTTP fetch. HTTP persistence resolves
   conflicts; websocket payloads are reconciliation hints.
7. Defer reconciliation while saving, refresh after authenticated reconnect, and
   use `WorkspaceDisconnectPollPlan` only while disconnected.

## Responsiveness and efficiency

- Sync-driven UI must remain high-performance, uniform, simple, and intuitive across mobile, tablet, and desktop, using reusable `frontend/lib/shared/` components.
- A successful local mutation must be visible in the same frame or next provider
  rebuild whenever the response or a deterministic delta is available; never block
  that visible update on a full workspace `GET`.
- Cross-session propagation is best-effort over WebSocket plus targeted HTTP
  reconciliation. Do not promise fixed network latency, ordering, or delivery.
- Prefer the smallest state patch. Blanket `ref.invalidate` or full workspace reload
  is a last resort when no correct targeted patch or refresh plan exists.
- Reuse shared burst coalescing. Add an explicit debounce only when measurement
  proves it reduces redundant work without creating perceptible staleness.
- Never navigate, show feedback, call `setState`, or mutate widgets from realtime
  handlers.

## Required verification for sync changes

- Unit-test optimistic/response delta appliers and new event-group membership.
- Controller-test success patching, cancel/failure behavior, and save-time deferral.
- Test event reconciliation and authenticated reconnect refresh.
- For cross-stack mutations, test backend event emission and a simulated second
  client updating without manual reload.

